there's sort of an old-fashioned feel to  everything from the way you order pizza  or the ice cream counter to the dining  room where you have something so unique  happening it really sets it apart from  anywhere else my name is brett valiant  and i play at oregon stop pizza in mesa  arizona a restaurant that serves pizza  and plays the world's largest wurlitzer  organ every night  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  oregon stock pizza started out in  arizona in 1972  [Music]  the whole idea of pizza and pipes  together started out in the 70s or late  60s and you know at one time there were  150 or so restaurants like this  throughout the country  it's a really good concept for people to  come in and just relax and have a good  time it's totally casual and it's easy  it's pizza  when somebody asks why in oregon you can  walk you know up the street anywhere and  find pianos this is truly a symphony  orchestra and band all rolled into one  and for people to come in and they see  one person sitting up at this giant  console it's something that you can't  find it anywhere  [Applause]  the organ here at oregon stop pizza is  the largest world's organ in the world  and the reason it's the largest is  because they've added lots of sets of  pipes to it over the years so it has 82  individual sets of pipes  the other thing that's really unique  about this instrument is that it has an  incredible number of percussions  everything from tuned percussions like  xylophones and marimbas and  glockenspiels to untuned percussions  drums and tambourines sound effects  pretty much any instrument ever  conceived for an orchestra is present  here  on this wurlitzer  there's nothing else like it in the  world  you can actually feel the power of the  organ just go through your whole body as  they play  the history and preservation of the  restaurant itself as well as  the art form of the theater organ is  really pretty special to me  people they'll say that it's so  old-fashioned in there and that's part  of the charm we are kind of stuck back  in that bygone era i think it's  important to really stick to that  because it is such a lost art form  i don't think we would exist if we  change anything really about this place  it's an experience and it's emotional  i'm very grateful that i get to be a  part  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

